-- name: Verify passed but no test touched alongside source
-- class: Consistency
-- metric: alignment_collect_d2
-- action: add or update tests alongside the source changes that shipped, or fix the project's verify command if it does not actually exercise tests
-- severity: WARN
-- guardrail: BOTH SIDES OF THIS DISAGREEMENT LIVE IN alignment-collect's OWN d2 RECORD -- spec docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-30-loopsmith-insight-data-platform-design.md:529's own parenthetical names alignment-collect d2 tests_touched_with_source_pct directly; alignment-collect.sh's own printf (lines 448-449) emits tests_touched_with_source_pct and test_command_known together in one object, so no join is needed or possible. POPULATION IS SCOPED TO PACKS THAT ACTUALLY CARRY A d2 BLOCK THAT IS A WELL-FORMED JSON OBJECT (json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d2') = 'OBJECT'), NOT MERELY "THE KEY EXTRACTS TO SOMETHING NON-NULL" -- POST-PR-REVIEW BLOCKING FIX (PR #203): the prior guard, json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d2') IS NOT NULL, was proven live to be a SECOND false-PASS trap, distinct from the schema-only one this design decision already fixed once: DuckDB's json_extract returns a non-SQL-NULL value for ANY present key regardless of JSON type -- a JSON null literal, an array, a bare string all satisfy IS NOT NULL; only a genuinely MISSING key returns SQL NULL. So that guard proved "the key exists", not "the key holds an object" -- a pack whose d2 is `null`, `[1,2,3]`, or a bare string read population 1 under it, while every field extraction below (test_command_known, tests_touched_with_source_pct) then extracts NULL and is wrapped in a CAST/IS TRUE comparison that quietly resolves to "nothing to flag", rendering a confident false PASS for a payload that was never actually shaped like d2 at all -- reproduced live against '{"dimensions":{"d2":null}}', '{"dimensions":{"d2":[1,2,3]}}' as {"severity":"PASS","evidence":[]} with population (1,) before this fix. FIXED by json_type(...) = 'OBJECT', mirroring coverage_review_missing.sql's own json_type(dp.config_json, '$.work.require_review') = 'BOOLEAN' idiom (that rule's own guardrail, same codebase, three files away) -- confirmed live: json_type returns 'OBJECT' for a real object, 'NULL' for a JSON null literal (still not SQL NULL), 'ARRAY', 'VARCHAR', and only Python None for a genuinely missing key, so this guard alone now separates all four. THE SAME json_type = 'OBJECT' CHECK IS ALSO ADDED TO THE EVIDENCE QUERY'S OWN WHERE CLAUSE, FOR DEFENSE IN DEPTH -- population and evidence are two independently-executed queries (evaluate.py runs the evidence query only when population > 0, but does not itself filter evidence rows by the population's own predicate), so a store mixing a well-formed pack with a malformed one would have population > 0 read correctly while the evidence query, unguarded, could still evaluate the malformed row's own CAST/COALESCE-softened NULLs; gating the evidence query on the same json_type check closes that gap directly rather than relying on the population check alone. FIRES ONLY ON ZERO TOUCHED TESTS (tests_touched_with_source_pct = 0), NEVER A PERCENTAGE CUTOFF -- an existence/emptiness comparison against 0, matching coverage_gate_absent.sql's/coverage_degraded_collector.sql's own established idiom, deliberately not a tuned magnitude. REQUIRES commits_with_source > 0 SO A TRIVIAL 0% IS NEVER MISREAD AS A REAL DISAGREEMENT -- alignment-collect.sh only computes tests_pct nonzero when COMMITS_WITH_SOURCE > 0 (line 396); a window with zero source commits emits tests_touched_with_source_pct: 0 trivially, and this rule must not fire on that. test_command_known IS TRUE, NEVER IS NOT FALSE -- a payload missing the key entirely reads NULL, and NULL IS TRUE is FALSE, correctly excluding it from evidence (three-valued logic, Design decision 5); NOTE FOR A FUTURE READER: a d2 record that is a well-formed object (passes this rule's own json_type population scoping) but omits test_command_known entirely therefore renders PASS, not ABSENT, at this rule's own grain -- unreachable through the real alignment-collect.sh writer today (it always emits test_command_known whenever it emits d2 at all, per its own printf line 448), but a future, less disciplined writer of a d2-shaped payload that copies this population-scoping idiom without also always supplying test_command_known would see that gap silently. MALFORMED-PAYLOAD DISCLOSURE, CORRECTED (post-PR-review, this line previously overclaimed): SYNTACTICALLY INVALID JSON (raw_payload is non-NULL text that will not parse as JSON at all) still makes every json_extract/json_type call in this rule's population and evidence queries raise DuckDB's InvalidInputException, crashing evaluate_rule rather than degrading to ABSENT -- the identical, unguarded json_extract(raw_payload, ...) pattern every one of the six already-shipped rules (and metrics 5/20/24/41) already shares; this fails LOUD, not silent, so it is not spec:534's forbidden collapse, and widening scope to guard every json_extract call across the catalog against syntactically-invalid JSON is out of this story's scope (see .sdlc/plans/120.md Risks). VALID JSON OF THE WRONG SHAPE (a d2 that parses fine but is null/array/scalar rather than an object) is a SEPARATE case, and -- unlike the syntactically-invalid case -- IS now caught, not merely disclosed: the json_type(...) = 'OBJECT' guard above excludes it from both population and evidence, rendering the honest ABSENT rather than crashing or silently passing. ORDER BY IS A TOTAL ORDER, UNLIKE coverage_degraded_collector.sql'S OWN PRECEDENT (non-blocking, flagged in review) -- fact_collector_pack has no primary key and collected_ts carries no uniqueness guarantee (store.py's DDL, re-checked), so two packs for one project written in the same instant would otherwise leave residual engine-whim ordering between them, the same accepted limitation 30.sql's own guardrail already names for an analogous LAG tiebreaker; here, `rowid` (DuckDB's own per-row identity, confirmed live to be queryable and distinct per row on both an in-memory and a file-backed connection) is appended as the final sort key, making this rule's own evidence order fully deterministic without touching the pre-existing catalog's shared, disclosed limitation.
-- population: SELECT count(*) FROM fact_collector_pack WHERE schema = 'alignment-collect/v1' AND json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d2') = 'OBJECT'
SELECT
    project_id,
    collected_ts,
    CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.commits_with_source') AS INTEGER) AS commits_with_source,
    CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d2.tests_touched_with_source_pct') AS INTEGER) AS tests_touched_with_source_pct,
    CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d2.test_command_known') AS BOOLEAN) AS test_command_known
FROM fact_collector_pack
WHERE schema = 'alignment-collect/v1'
  AND json_type(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d2') = 'OBJECT'
  AND CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d2.test_command_known') AS BOOLEAN) IS TRUE
  AND COALESCE(CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d1.commits_with_source') AS INTEGER), 0) > 0
  AND CAST(json_extract(raw_payload, '$.dimensions.d2.tests_touched_with_source_pct') AS INTEGER) = 0
ORDER BY project_id, collected_ts, rowid
